plan Persistence
title   Persistence: durable state without replaying work
status  proposed
goal
    An app can retain selected drafts and confirmed data across reloads within one app and
    scope. Restore never repeats a remote write, exposes partial saved state or undoes a live
    turn.
outcomes
    O1  Configuration and storage
        runtimeSetup({ app, scope, services?, persistence? }) enables persistence. Without it,
        Flow acquires no storage and performs no restore, capture or storage I/O. Persistence
        requires the setup's scope (@plan.Runtime.O2); its absence is a type error and rejects
        untyped setups before construction, so saved data never lands in a shared partition.
        There is no app-wide or manual version: each record carries its own versions and saved
        shape, checked per record and per definition at the boot (@O5). Different apps and
        scopes never read, overwrite or delete each other's data.

        Enabled persistence requires an Effect KeyValueStore service from effect/persistence;
        missing provision is a type error. Untyped missing provision fails ready with
        StorageFailure. construct() performs no I/O. Storage is available before restore; saved
        data is validated before actors start. Failed bootstrap exposes no partially restored
        live app and releases acquired resources under @plan.Runtime.O2.

        Flow supplies layerIndexedDb({ database? }) as a KeyValueStore Layer; database defaults
        to a name derived from the app id, so apps sharing an origin never collide. It preserves
        saved values, releases its connection on disposal and reports storage failures. Authors
        can supply another KeyValueStore without changing persistence selection. Records are
        stored as JSON strings. Public routes follow @plan.Package.O1; isolated Story storage
        follows @plan.Testing.O3.

        Superseded: "runtimeSetup({ app, scope, services?, persistence?: { version } })" and
        "version is a required nonempty string and matches exactly" are replaced by automatic
        checks with no app-wide or manual version: per definition (the user's Q1 A), decided per
        record (the runner's ruling, OD54 (8)) (C211).
    O2  What survives reload
        Selection defaults to false. persist is a property of the machine, declared on its
        definition: definition({ id, persist: true, ... }); refs carry no persist, so every ref
        of an identity agrees. A local actor of a persisted machine (createActor, useActor) is
        not persisted and writes no record. resource({ persist: true }) selects confirmed
        resource data independently of actor selection. Transactions and streams have no
        persist option.

        A persisted actor's state, memory, exact context bindings, issues and pending mapped
        outcomes survive reload, together with schedule deadlines (keyed schedules' per-key
        timers by key with dueAt), restartOn baselines, actor revisions, each history target's
        last active child, resumable fragment slices (frozen ones with their frozen schedules and
        the parked Interrupted endings of lookups the freeze cut), .each copies by key with
        their incarnation counters, a writing placement's reset value with its slice, and, for
        each placement, the last value it mapped to a value outcome. After reload, value fires
        only when the confirmed value differs from that mapped value, so a fresh lookup of
        unchanged data never overwrites a restored draft. A stream's bookmark (its partition per
        operation and key: at, filled, and a latest row's last and heard) persists with its
        slice, and a frozen slice keeps it; the position field's Schema must pass this outcome's
        type rule. Memory uses its definition's Schema; selected resource results require their
        output Schema. Persisted Schemas must encode and decode synchronously without services;
        incompatible Schemas are a type error. Unsupported values fail encoding instead of being
        coerced, omitted or stringified. The actor's identity is encoded with its Schemas
        (@plan.Runtime.O3); a key is stored as canonical text, so a bigint 1n restores as "$1n".

        Write lanes are Runtime records of the actor's identity, never inside the actor's
        record: every non-Idle lane of a persisted identity, bound or unbound, stored under
        (identity with its version, contract id, key) with its Attempts, each with its retryCount
        and whether it was acknowledged, the creating incarnation's subject path (strings), the
        releasing mark (a flag), and parked endings, raw (the contract's outcome decoded by its
        output and error Schemas, plus Flow's reasons, the Attempt and the lane state after it),
        mapped through the current definition's outcomes at delivery. Pending mapped outcomes
        include endings of a persisted identity's lanes admitted while no incarnation is bound, and
        its NotSent { Disposed }. A local identity's lanes never persist; one-off read lanes are not
        persisted.

        Only confirmed shared data survives; optimistic values and predictions never become a
        saved baseline. Entity projection and uncertainty repair follow @plan.EntityDomains.O1.
        Queued events (including admitted public events not yet processed), commands, executable
        work, services and stream emission buffers do not survive. Pending mapped outcomes
        survive for once-only delivery. Context selections are recomputed from the saved
        providers, never restored as independent overrides.

        Superseded: "actorRef(machine, id, { persist: true }) selects a stable shared actor" is
        replaced by persist on the definition, default false (C120; C160; C175). "Transactions
        have no persist option: pending writes follow their actor." is replaced by Runtime lane
        records (C17; C44). "A stream's persist option retains its latest value only when its
        owning actor also persists." is replaced by the bookmark partition (C86; C93). "for each
        activity binding" reads each placement (C40). The fragment, history, keyed-schedule and
        lane-record contents are added (fragments row; P5; C60).
    O3  Complete captures and writes
        A capture contains a consistent view of selected actors, their lanes and shared
        confirmed data: it sees only whole publications, never part of a turn or an actor
        without its store writes. It never starts operations, emits context events or changes
        live state. It includes every non-Idle lane of a persisted identity, bound or unbound; a
        releasing mark is captured no later than the actor record that drops the incarnation. An
        ended actor's record is encoded in its end step and kept. disposeActor removes the
        actor's record, keeps its non-Idle lanes as unbound lane records, and flushes the
        removal at once; disposeActor and forget stop a send's restore only once their removal is
        acknowledged. forget removes the identity's record and its parked endings (as evidence)
        and drops no hold, except a hold whose operation no longer exists (HoldForgotten,
        proposed name) (@plan.Runtime.O6). A pending mapped outcome settled before the final
        capture, and never-sent work ended NotSent { Disposed }, are delivered after reload; at
        Runtime disposal an unknown keeps its head Held on disk and a definite ending drops it.
        A record reset at the boot keeps its writes in flight: lanes, holds and parked endings
        stay unbound until an adopting ensure. Dormant lazy records follow @plan.LazyFeatures.O3.

        Every selected consumer requires its exact transitive context providers to be durable. A
        missing, local or non-durable provider rejects the whole capture, preserves the previous
        complete scope and reports NonDurableContextProvider with every failing context path and
        provider identity. Flow never substitutes a provider or silently drops the consumer.
        Incomplete context propagation delays capture and retries automatically when settled; it
        never writes a mixed or partial context view.

        A scope is saved atomically: after interruption or a torn write, reload sees one complete
        saved capture, falling back to the last good capture when necessary. Older writes never
        replace newer committed state. Every actor and lane record carries Flow's record format
        (1 to 4). Plain memory coalesces for 5 s and is flushed at once on visibilitychange to
        hidden and on pagehide; a batch that touches a lane or a send, or a disposeActor removal,
        is written at once with everything dirty, so a lane never outruns its actor; a first
        send's capture and acknowledgement are one batch with the turn that committed it; a
        hidden page writes at once. The durability required by @O6 cannot be coalesced away. A
        stream partition is written once per changed last. A scope admits at most 4,096 records
        and 16 MiB of record data, including dormant records; over either bound, a capture drops
        the oldest ended records that equal a fresh start, with evidence, and if that is not
        enough the capture is rejected and its evidence names the oldest ended identities, so the
        app can forget them; a record that holds anything a user did is never dropped. Shared
        value bounds follow @plan.Inspection.O2; encoding failure preserves the last good
        capture.

        The store's contract (agent-judged): batches are atomic and commit in issue order; per key
        a later write wins, whatever order the answers come in; one plain write per key is out
        at a time; an awaited write waits for any plain batch of its actor still out and carries
        what that batch owed; a batch answers landed only if every request in it succeeded. A
        write the disk has not answered is pending, never "not sent": evidence StorageSilent names
        it after 10 s and decides nothing; nothing bounds a wait on storage (a stated limit). Page
        hide, visibility becoming hidden and Runtime disposal request a flush; a frozen page runs
        no task, deadline, backoff or capture until a thaw, and a discard is a kill with no
        pagehide. Browser exit flushing is best effort; on Safari a tab close loses the last
        batch, so the loss window is the 5 s interval. A record names the boot that wrote it and
        restore's id floor is the largest it reads, so an id on disk is never minted again.
        Disposal waits for the last write before releasing storage and ownership, subject to
        bounded disposal in @plan.Runtime.O6. A write still pending when the disposal budget ends
        reports StorageFailure rather than hanging or claiming a completed flush.

        Superseded: "Explicitly disposed durable actors disappear from the next successful
        capture" is replaced: a disposed actor's non-Idle lanes stay as unbound lane records
        (C45; C159). "a persisted actor whose retain expired keeps its saved record
        (@plan.Runtime.O3)" is replaced by an ended actor's record encoded in its end step (C106).
        "Exceeding either bound rejects the whole capture without eviction." is replaced by
        dropping records equal to a fresh start first (C106). "Ordinary writes are coalesced to
        the newest waiting state" is replaced by the 5 s interval with immediate lane batches
        (C131). "During Runtime disposal, a commit that settles before the final capture is saved
        as a pending mapped outcome and delivered after reload." is kept, amended by the head's
        disk state at disposal (C45; C176). Record formats, forget and resets are added (C173;
        C178; C210; C212).
    O4  Restore without replay
        ready() restores a compatible scope before actor work starts. Restored records stay
        records until an ensure, a subscription or a send reaches them, except an identity whose
        captured lanes owe it an ending (a lane InFlight at capture, or a parked ending), which is
        restored live at ready and kept as work in flight until those turns run, so offline
        queued writes resume after a reload without each screen being opened. A restored record
        is a new incarnation, with new ids for the actor and every fragment incarnation it
        restores. Ensuring a restored actor takes no input (@plan.Runtime.O3). Restore never runs
        creation again or replays old input or turns.

        Saved context bindings select the same providers and establish a silent baseline,
        without an onContext event. A writing placement whose re-taken reset value differs from
        the recorded one resets as a pending reset, the first turn after restore, before the lane
        deliveries. Then, per lane in creation order, the incarnation receives the parked endings
        in saved order; on a reload each queued commit's NotSent { Restored } first, then a Held
        head's unknown { Restored } once (unless its outcome was saved), so the ending that
        leaves a hold comes last; an Attempt acknowledged once ends unknown { Restored } even if
        its re-send was waiting or it was never invoked; a head whose own ending is parked
        restores as that parked ending, delivered once, never also unknown { Restored }. These are
        delivered after readiness, before new external events and before continuing placements
        reconcile. A kill before a first send's batch lands restores the state before that turn,
        with no lane and no ending. Finite resource requests never resume their old execution.

        Restored resource data starts stale. A live placement can start a fresh policy-driven
        lookup; key-only entries remain passive until a live placement supplies input. Input is
        never reconstructed from a cache key. Restored unobserved entries start gcTime at ready;
        resource freshness and input selection follow @plan.Operations.O2. Hydration precedence
        follows @plan.ServerRendering.O5.

        Restored schedules and hidden-tab resume follow @plan.Machines.O5. A live placement
        re-opens from its restored input: a latest feed restores last and its baseline, and an
        every feed restores its bookmark with the slice, then starts a fresh session after saved
        outcomes drain; no old emission replays. Late results from old work cannot affect
        restored work. Lazy adoption follows @plan.LazyFeatures.O3; capture and restore never
        load lazy code.

        Superseded: "Restored stable actors are active without another ensure" is replaced by
        records staying records until reached (C106). "ensureActor returns the restored actor and
        ignores fresh input" is replaced: no ensure takes input (C120; C144). "Their retain clock
        starts at ready." is removed with retain (C106). "Saved mapped outcomes are delivered
        once, in saved order after readiness" gains the lane order and pending resets (C46; C62;
        C151; C179). "A crash after acknowledgment restores unknown" is amended by a parked ending
        restoring as itself (C17; C167). "Running streams retain their selected latest value and
        count" becomes the latest and every restore rows (C94). "Terminal streams stay terminal
        until their activity starts again." is replaced by declared reconnect (C87; C94).
    O5  Honest failures and forgetting
        Restore is per record. Corrupt scope data that no record can be read from falls back to
        the last good capture, or starts fresh; ready succeeds. Each older record format reaches
        the current one by a pure step at the boot, before the release's checks; a newer, null or
        non-integer record format is quarantined, its lane still holding by its id. An actor
        record that does not decode is quarantined: it reads lifecycle quarantined with its
        issue, and an ensure starts fresh, reports RecordUndecodable (proposed name) and adopts
        the identity's lanes; the quarantined bytes are overwritten when the fresh actor first
        saves (the runner's ruling). Lane records decode by their contract's input pick, independent
        of the actor record, so a fresh incarnation adopts them in its initial leaf; this is not a
        partial restore of the actor. A position inside a stream partition that fails to decode
        reads { at: undefined }; a partition failing its own shape discards the scope. v1 has no
        migration API: a compatible change migrates by the field's Schema alone.

        Versions. An actor's identity version and each operation key's fingerprint compare every
        field's type side (checks by representation) and encoded side, ignoring other
        annotations, under Flow's format-tagged encodings (@plan.Runtime.O3). A saved actor record
        of another identity version is an old-version record: listed at ready (OldVersionActor,
        proposed name), never renamed, removed by forget; its lanes are re-keyed at the boot to
        key#identityTag (LaneRekeyed evidence, proposed name), kept, blocking nothing, and listed
        UnboundLane (proposed name) at each boot; a release that makes the old identity current
        again hands the key's lane back unless it is taken; the old identity's unknown hold has no
        user exit and ends only by its own ending (a stated limit). A lane of an older key
        fingerprint is an old-version hold, reported at each boot as the issue OldVersionLane, and
        declines its operation's commits for that identity NotSent { OldVersion }
        (@plan.Operations.O3).

        Saved shapes. Each definition's records are reset automatically (the user's Q1 A: per
        definition, no manual or app-wide version), decided per record (the runner's ruling) when
        the saved shape (state ids, memory fields with their Schema versions, fragments' under their
        id) differs incompatibly: a kept field under another Schema version, or a saved leaf,
        child or history target gone. The boot removes such a record, retired or not, with its
        deadlines and stopped reads, and reports one RecordShapeChanged (proposed name) per
        definition with its count; the identity check comes first; a record with no shape stamp
        never resets. Any structural Schema change to a stored field resets the record, a
        harmless validation change included (the runner's ruling; a stated price). Its writes in
        flight are kept (@O3), and each boot reports a head whose identity's record does not
        read, or was reset, as HoldWithoutRecord (proposed name). A field the record has and this
        release does not declare is dropped with the issue FieldDropped (proposed name) once the
        record is known to restore, and the record is rewritten without it at that boot (the one
        exception to untouched records not being rewritten). A renamed field is a drop plus an
        add, and a changed default is not in the version (stated limits).

        Lanes. A lane whose key no longer decodes, whose operation is gone, or whose recorded
        path the placement cannot produce is quarantined: it keeps its id and hold, a commit on
        its key declines NotSent { Rejected }, the record stays, Inspection lists it and each
        boot reports it, and a cancel on that key clears it (with no operation, forget, which
        drops its hold with HoldForgotten). There is no key-equality adoption and no orphaned
        handler. A lane whose operation name this release lacks moves to the one write operation
        over its contract (OpRenamed, proposed name); a move onto a lane record already on disk,
        or onto another move of the same boot, is refused with LaneRekeyCollision (proposed name),
        the mover quarantined with its hold and nothing overwritten. Saved actor records for a
        machine id that no eager or lazy entry declares are discarded with a diagnostic; their
        lane records are quarantined as lanes whose operation is gone (the runner's ruling,
        reading the user's quarantine decision as covering a removed machine). A dormant record
        that fails to decode when its feature is adopted is quarantined like any undecodable
        record (the runner's ruling, following the undecodable-record rule) (@plan.LazyFeatures.O3).

        A storage read failure reports StorageFailure and disables persistence for that Runtime,
        so unread saved data is never overwritten; a send to a persisted actor before boot
        queues until boot, within the boot bound, and past it or on a failed read the queue is
        flushed with StorageFailure and a late answer is ignored. Missing required storage
        remains a setup failure. A failed write preserves live committed state and the last good
        saved capture. It never retroactively fails a committed actor turn. A later publication
        retries the newest state. Encoding never fails a turn: memory that cannot encode skips its
        capture batch and keeps the last good one, named UnencodableValue (@plan.Inspection.O2).
        Failures reach Runtime diagnostic observers even without mounted durable actors; a
        failing inspection sink cannot interrupt persistence. Error vocabulary and remediation
        follow @plan.Diagnostics.O2.

        runtime.dispose({ forget: true }) waits for this scope's data to be made unrestorable and
        deleted before releasing ownership. Failed deletion reports StorageFailure and never
        claims successful forgetting. Ordinary disposal retains saved data. A Runtime running
        without persistence performs no forget I/O. Disposal failures follow @plan.Runtime.O6.

        Superseded: "Incompatible versions or identities and corrupt scope data report
        RestoreDiscarded and start fresh" and "Flow never partially restores an invalid scope."
        are replaced by per-record restore, quarantine, old-version records and saved-shape resets
        (C47; C120; C143; C210; C211). "Compatible shape changes may use Schema decode
        transformations; v1 has no migration API." is kept (C211). "saved actor records for a
        machine id that no eager or lazy entry declares are discarded with a diagnostic" is kept
        for the actor records; their lane records follow the quarantine (C47; C152). "A dormant
        record that fails to decode ... discards only that record, with a diagnostic; the feature
        opens fresh" is replaced by quarantine and a fresh ensure (C143). "Encoding failure never
        fails the turn: the turn commits, the whole write is skipped with the codec's code" now
        names UnencodableValue (C154). P19's orphaned handler is cut (C152). Versions, record
        formats, old-version lanes, renames and dropped fields are added (C135; C161; C174; C181;
        C213; C214); the boot queue is added (C155).
    O6  Durable attempts before remote writes
        All sends of a write are one Attempt (same id, same input), and attempt.retryCount counts
        the re-sends. A persisted identity's remote commit cannot invoke its adapter until
        storage acknowledges its Attempt, once per Attempt, per lane record, needing no actor,
        including id and encoded input; the acknowledgement is one batch with the capture of the
        turn that committed it, and is written in one capture with the records the Attempt
        predicts (prediction values are never persisted). A persisted re-send waits for its lane
        record's capture, not a new acknowledgement. If acknowledgement fails, the adapter is not
        called and the commit ends NotSent { StorageRefused } with StorageFailure evidence; a
        re-send whose lane-record capture fails returns to Held and delivers unknown {
        StorageRefused }. Transient storage failure never silently downgrades a selected actor's
        commit to an undurable write. A domain write's lane record also saves its basedOn per
        record when a version was known at mint, and says sent once re-written after the send; a
        doubt restored from a record written before its send trusts no base; predicted keys carry
        the fields each prediction touched.

        A crash after acknowledgement restores unknown { Restored } carrying the same Attempt,
        since the Runtime cannot know whether invocation began; a head whose own ending is parked
        restores as that ending. A saved queued label alone is insufficient: dispatch may have
        happened after that saved capture. Flow never automatically repeats a remote write.
        Already saved mapped outcomes are delivered rather than replaced by unknown. Re-sending is
        O.x.retry(k?) (@plan.Operations.O3). A later Runtime never reuses an Attempt identity for
        an unrelated write.

        Non-persisted actors, including a Runtime refused persistence under @O7, have no attempt
        durability and lose their workflow on crash; a local actor's write sent before a reload
        has no lane afterwards, so its ending is not seen and its hold is lost (a stated limit;
        the remedy is a persisted identity or a version precondition). They may still execute
        remote commits; there is no cross-tab exactly-once guarantee.

        Superseded: "A persisted actor's remote commit cannot invoke its adapter until storage
        acknowledges its Attempt, including id and encoded input." is amended: once per Attempt,
        per lane record, one batch with its turn (P15; C48; C128). "If acknowledgment fails, the
        adapter is not called; the occurrence settles through interrupt with StorageFailure
        evidence." is replaced by NotSent { StorageRefused } (C48). "A crash after acknowledgment
        restores unknown carrying the same Attempt unless durable evidence proves the adapter
        could not have run." becomes unknown { Restored }, or the parked ending (C17; C167).
        "Explicit retry(attempt) and known-settled behavior follow @plan.Operations.O3." becomes
        O.x.retry(k?) (C23). basedOn and predicted-key fields are added (C200).
    O7  A second tab runs without persistence
        At ready(), a persistence-enabled Runtime requests an immediately available Web Lock for
        its app and scope before accessing storage. The page holds the lock while it is the live
        page: on pagehide with persisted, after its own flush, the host releases the lock and
        leaves no batch open; on pageshow with persisted it reloads, which is a new boot. It holds
        ownership through final flush or forgetting and cleanup; failed bootstrap releases
        ownership. Other apps and scopes remain independent.

        If a disposing Runtime in the same document owns the scope, ready waits for its release.
        If another tab or Runtime owns that scope, this Runtime runs independently without
        restore, capture, writes or deletion and reports SecondTabNoPersistence; a persisted
        identity's commit there ends NotSent { NoPersistence } and its transition runs. It stays
        in that mode even after the owner closes. A new Runtime may try again. There is no tab
        sharing or handover. Where navigator.locks is absent, persistence proceeds without an
        ownership check. If an available lock request fails, report StorageFailure and run
        without persistence. The capture that removes a cancelled Attempt's acknowledgement
        queues behind that acknowledgement (one writer per scope); an acknowledgement stored
        before a crash restores as unknown { Restored } even if this Runtime had cancelled the
        Attempt.

        Superseded: "requests an immediately available Web Lock for its app and scope,
        independent of version" drops "independent of version" with the app-wide version (C211);
        the back-forward cache rule (C131), NoPersistence (C130) and the cancel capture order
        (C85) are added.
decisions
    D1  Persisted storage is faithful
        Saved records hold values exactly as committed, including fields and values marked
        sensitive. Storage belongs to the user's own browser profile and scope, and restore must
        reproduce drafts unchanged. Sensitivity governs only data that leaves the Runtime:
        exported evidence (@plan.Inspection.O5) and server-rendering payloads
        (@plan.ServerRendering.O3). Clearing a scope is runtime.dispose({ forget: true }).
verification
    V1  Type tests reject missing storage and invalid persisted Schemas under @O1 and @O2.
    V2  Tests prove default-off selection, inert construction and atomic bootstrap under @O1.
    V3  Tests prove complete context capture, automatic retry, lane records and disposed-actor
        lanes under @O3.
    V4  Reload tests prove restored identity, ordered lane deliveries and no work replay under
        @O4, including restore-capture-restore and hydrate-persist-reload-hydrate round trips.
    V5  Crash tests prove Attempt acknowledgement before dispatch and honest unknown under @O6.
    V6  Fault tests prove atomic fallback, bounds, quarantine, saved-shape resets and
        preservation of live state under @O3 and @O5.
    V7  Browser tests prove a second tab running without persistence, the back-forward cache rule
        and no-lock persistence under @O7.
    V8  Tests prove confirmed-only restore under @O2, dormant retention under @O3 and hydration
        precedence under @O4.
    V9  Browser tests prove the 5 s capture, flush on hide, bounded disposal and honest
        forgetting under @O3 and @O5.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Storage key layout and identity encoding. Record chunking, capture bookkeeping and
        generation counters. Manifest headers, integrity checks and retained-generation layout.
        Write scheduling within a publishing task. IndexedDB transaction cancellation and
        connection cleanup mechanics. Diagnostic payload bounds and encoding details.
        Cross-runtime codec vectors and exact shared codec limits. Fault-injection
        infrastructure and browser test matrix.
